// Passing a function to a higher-order function by name is not the same as passing a lambda. When
// the named function carries implicit parameters, the checker rewrites the reference into a wrapper
// lambda that supplies them (`fn p q -> step p {Drop t} q`). That wrapper is the only form the
// function takes at the indirect call, so it is what owns -- and must release -- every argument the
// caller hands it.
//
// It released nothing. The wrapper is re-checked with move recording suppressed (the moves were
// already recorded for the reference it replaced), and `drop_elaboration_active` reads that same
// flag, so drop elaboration was off for the wrapper's entire body: no drop scope, no parameter
// drops, no glue at all. Every accumulator `foldl` handed it was orphaned -- the 767 KB leak in
// `examples/codegen/shared.an`, whose `foldl (iota size) RbTree.Empty insert` passes `insert`
// (three implicit capabilities) by name.
//
// Nothing here is about closure captures: the wrapper captures nothing. It is about the wrapper's
// own parameters.
import Std.Stream.foldl, iota, stream_fn

shared type Acc = n: I32

impl drop_acc: Drop Acc with
    drop (a: mut Acc) =
        print "  drop "
        println a.n

// `_x: t` is an owned generic the body drops, so `step` needs `{Drop t}` -- an implicit parameter,
// which is exactly what makes `foldl (iota 3) (Acc 0) step` build the coercion wrapper. `old` is a
// concretely-`shared` by-value parameter, so `step` itself borrows it and never releases it: the
// wrapper is the only thing that can.
step (old: Acc) (_x: t) {Drop t}: Acc = Acc (old.n + 1)

main () =
    // Each step supersedes the accumulator, so every intermediate must die here, in order, as the
    // wrapper releases what `foldl` moved into it. The last one survives to be returned.
    total = foldl (iota 3) (Acc 0) step
    println total.n
    println "end"

// args: --delete-binary --no-color --auto-drop
// expected stdout:
//   drop 0
//   drop 1
//   drop 2
// 3
// end
//   drop 3
